WEBVTT

NOTE
Automatic English captions. Generated locally and checked with additional audio decodes; not fully audited by listening.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.820
A knowledge base is only as good as the
documents inside it. If those documents

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:11.560
live somewhere else, in Drive, Notion,
GitHub, or another source, uploading them

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:16.880
once is not enough. The source changes,
and your agent's knowledge needs to change

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:22.160
with it. Connectors keep the knowledge
base tied to where the work already lives,

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:27.700
so that answers stay grounded in the
current material. Let me show you how

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:32.880
connectors work. This knowledge base holds
17 documents, and every one of them was

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:37.260
uploaded by hand. The moment a source
changes, the copy in here starts to drift.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.320
Instead of re -uploading, you add a
connector, and Sim opens a picker of

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:43.780
sources it can sync from.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.300
Pick Notion, and the connector asks how to
read your workspace.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:56.640
You authorize the account once, and Sim
keeps the connection alive from there.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:02.800
Then you choose what to sync, the whole
workspace, one database, or a single page,

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:07.600
and its children. Here, the docs page. And
you choose how often it checks. From every

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.640
hour down to manual only, this one syncs
daily.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.600
Connect and sync, and the connector lands
beside your knowledge base and starts its

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:17.600
first import.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:25.060
12 pages stream in from Notion. The base
grows from 17 documents to 29, and the

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:30.820
first sync is written into the history.
Synced pages are processed exactly like

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:33.680
uploads, split into chunks and indexed for
search.

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:39.960
From here, the connector checks on its
schedule, and most days, there is nothing

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:40.960
to do.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:47.460
When someone edits a page in Notion, the
next sync catches it, and only that

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:48.940
document is reprocessed.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:55.640
And when a page is deleted at the source,
it leaves the base too, so nothing stale

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:56.640
is left behind.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.980
The payoff shows up in your workflows.
When an agent searches this base, it

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.540
answers from the passage that's synced
this morning.

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:12.440
That is what a connector buys you. A
knowledge base that keeps itself current.
